// The conformance corpus: what tutuca script MEANS, as data.
//
// One AST, TWO backends: the MoonBit emitter (`tscript/emit_mbt`) and the wasm
// compiler (`tgc/emit`). There were three, and the third — an interpreter
// that ran a card where it stood — has been removed now that the compiler
// covers what it did. A language with two implementations and no shared corpus
// does not have one semantics, it has two, and the difference shows up as a
// component that behaves differently depending on how it was built.
//
// So the cases are DATA and nothing here runs them. Each backend writes an
// adapter — the emitter generates a module and drives it through `@harness`,
// the compiler compiles each case into a card and drives the instantiated
// module from node — and a case that one backend passes and another does not is
// the thing the corpus exists to catch.
//
// LOSING the interpreter cost the corpus something worth naming: it was the
// implementation you could read in an afternoon, and it was where a case's
// answer came from when somebody wrote one. Both tables state their answers
// outright now, which they had to for the compiler's adapter to exist at all —
// a compiled card can only be driven from node, and JavaScript cannot re-derive
// a MoonBit `Debug`.
//
// Which is why an EFFECT is structural here (`CaseEffect`) and used to be a
// printed line. The line was chosen so that no backend's spelling became the
// language's — a fair worry, and `kind` being a plain word rather than an enum
// arm keeps it — but it meant the values had to be recovered by running an
// implementation, and the one that got run was the interpreter.
import {
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/core" @tutuca,
}
